# Compact Reference Files

This repository keeps two layers every rule skill document so humans can write review readable prose while agent works minimal token-efficient copy

- `*.mdh` (markdown-human) source truth Write normal readable English here This file edit
- The Cursor-facing file (`.mdc` rules `.md` skills) generated token-minimal ("caveman") Never edit hand run generator instead

## Why two layers?

Agents consume many files single context window Compact files reduce token usage without losing meaning Humans need full prose understand intent spot errors maintain content over time The two layers serve different audiences without duplicating effort

## Generation map

The generator derives output path location source:

- `*.mdh` under `.cursor/rules/` compiles `*.mdc` (a Cursor rule YAML frontmatter header)
- any other `*.mdh` compiles `*.md` (a skill file reference file or tool doc)

## What preserved verbatim

The squeeze only touches plain prose words These kept exactly written:

- YAML frontmatter so rule `description`/`globs` skill `name`/`description` remain unchanged
- Fenced code blocks inline `code` spans
- Markdown links tables
- Identifiers, CamelCase names ALLCAPS constants symbols numbers
- Negations logic words: no not never if then when use make put keep move

Articles, copulas filler prepositions dropped Plain prose words lowercased

## Workflow

1. Edit relevant `*.mdh` source file
2. Run generator repo root so all siblings refreshed
3. Commit both `*.mdh` source generated file together

```bash
py .cursor/tools/cavemanize.py
```

Verify no output stale (useful pre-commit check or CI):

```bash
py .cursor/tools/cavemanize.py --check
```

Process single source file:

```bash
py .cursor/tools/cavemanize.py --file .cursor/skills/model/SKILL.mdh
```

## Reading rule agents

When both compact human files exist read `.mdc` or `.md` during normal agent work Open `.mdh` only when explicitly editing human-readable prose or when compact content not sufficient complete task safely
